# Global instructions (environment: Docker Sandbox / sbx)

## The environment is an isolated microVM (sbx)

This session runs inside a Docker Sandbox: a microVM with its own Linux kernel,
separated from the host by a hypervisor.

The working directory is bind-mounted from the host and visible there.
Everything else in the VM is ephemeral: it lives as long as the sandbox does and
is wiped when the sandbox is recreated.

## A blocked domain is a full stop, not a task

Outbound traffic goes through an allowlist. A domain that is not on it looks
like "DNS does not resolve", "connection refused", or a dropped connection at
the proxy — and it is NOT a reason to start fixing code.

**The moment it is clear that a domain is blocked, stop and tell the user.**
Do not invent a way around it: no stubbing out network calls, no swapping the
client for a mock, no disabling checks, no editing someone else's code
"temporarily". Even if you honestly revert the edit afterwards, a run made
against a stub verified nothing, and the user finds that out far later than they
should have.

Say it plainly: which host is needed, which step it blocked, and that one
command on the host opens it. Give that command with the host filled in, ready
to paste:

```powershell
sbx policy allow network --sandbox ${SANDBOX_NAME} <host>[:<port>]
```

A hostname opens exactly that host. To open a domain together with all its
subdomains, write `**.example.com`.

Without `--sandbox` the rule is global and opens the domain for every sandbox —
only offer that if the user asks for it.

Then it is the user's call: open the domain, point you at another environment,
or accept a run without that piece. The one thing to do on your own is finish
everything that does not depend on that host, and state explicitly what was left
unverified.

The host's GitHub token is NOT forwarded into the sandbox automatically. Until
gh auth is set up through a kit (serviceAuth/proxy), `gh` will answer "not
logged in" — that is expected, and NOT a reason to run `gh auth login`. Read-only
commands that need no token work.

## FS

- **`sed -i` in the working directory drops the file mode to `0600`** — and
  tests then fail for no real reason: a container runs under a different uid and
  simply cannot read the file (`PermissionError: [Errno 13]` on import).
  Reproduces reliably, and only on the bind-mount: the same `sed -i` in `/tmp`
  leaves `0644`. So after any bulk edit with `sed -i` (renaming imports and the
  like) run `find src tests -name "*.py" ! -perm -o+r -exec chmod 644 {} +`
  before the tests. Scripts you mount into a container with `-v` also have to be
  `chmod 644`, or the container cannot read them.
- **`mv` of a directory occasionally fails with `Permission denied`** if it
  contains something a container wrote recently (typically `__pycache__` after a
  test run). The modes look fine, and from the host the file shows up under your
  own uid. Seen twice, 0 out of 5 on a retry — a race on the bind-mount, not a
  rule. Delete `__pycache__`
  (`find <path> -name __pycache__ -type d -exec rm -rf {} +`) and repeat the
  `mv`; no need to hunt for a cause in the code.
- **Do not blame the bind-mount for edits that suddenly rolled back.**
  Work happening on the host side is
  the likelier explanation. Check `git log` / `git status` before explaining it
  with the filesystem.

## Git state

Every user message carries a `<git-state>` block — `git status --short --branch`
and the last five commits as of that message. That is the current state of the
repository; any earlier git output in the conversation is stale. No need to
re-run status for the general picture, only for details (a diff, the full log, a
specific commit).

## Python projects

Run everything through `uv run` — do not activate a virtualenv. A venv cannot
live on the workspace mount, so `uv` is wrapped to keep each project's
environment under `~/.venvs`; `uv run` resolves it on the fly. There is no
`./.venv` in the repo. For example: `uv run ruff check src`.
